<title>USA: Wet conditions delay Idaho grain harvest.</title>
<headline>Wet conditions delay Idaho grain harvest.</headline>
<dateline>BOISE, Idaho 1997-08-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The following is the weekly crop summary from the Idaho Agricultural Statistics Service for the week ending August 3.</p>
<p>	    JULY ENDS WITH WET, HUMID WEATHER</p>
<p>Wet, humid conditions delayed grain and hay harvest. High humidity and wind driven storms are making conditions ideal for the spread of late blight.  Fremont county has had positive reports of late blight. Bingham and Cassia counties have received numerous new reports of fields testing positive. All weather stations received precipitation last week except for Lewiston. Average temperatures were near normal. The Idaho Agricultural Statistics Service reports that farmers had an average of 5.8 days suitable for fieldwork during the past week. Agricultural activities for the week ending August 3 included, inspecting and spraying potatoes for late blight, preparing machinery for grain, potato, and sugarbeet harvest, trying to harvesting hay, barley, winter and spring wheat, weed control, and irrigating.</p>
<p>			    CROP REPORT</p>
<p>Potato conditions continue to decline due to late blight and hollow heart diseases along with green peach aphid infestation.  Winds the past week increased lodging in grain fields across the State. Grain harvest remains behind average due to rain delays. Alfalfa harvest has also been delayed with some damage occurring due to rain. Topsoil moisture levels were reported to be 18% short, 77% adequate and 5% surplus. Apples are in good condition and are starting to turn color.</p>
<p>			  LIVESTOCK REPORT</p>
<p>For the week ending August 3, no livestock problems were reported. Cattle and sheep remain in good to excellent condition.</p>
